{* =========================================================================
   aLatteX Demo 3 - Latte inside chunks and snippets

   The question this page answers: "my chunk contains {$var} and it comes out
   on the page as the four characters {$var}. Why?"

   Because aLatteX processes the template, and only the template. Evolution
   CMS expands {{chunks}} and [[snippets]] in parseDocumentSource(), which
   runs after Latte has finished and does not call Latte again. A chunk is
   therefore plain markup as far as Latte is concerned.

   Three ways out, shown side by side below.
   ========================================================================= *}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{evoSetting('manager_language') ?: 'en'}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>{$pagetitle} - aLatteX demo</title>
</head>
<body class="alx alx-chunks">

{{aLatteXDemoHeader}}

<section id="literal">
    <h3>1. What actually happens by default</h3>
    <p>
        aLatteXDemoCard is a chunk written in Latte. Included the ordinary
        way, its tags reach the browser as text:
    </p>

    <h4>Via the EVO tag</h4>
    <pre class="alx-literal">{{aLatteXDemoCard}}</pre>

    <h4>Via evoChunk(), during Latte's own pass</h4>
    {* evoChunk() does run during the Latte pass - but it returns the chunk as
       finished Html, it does not compile it. Same literal result. *}
    <pre class="alx-literal">{evoChunk('aLatteXDemoCard')}</pre>
</section>

<section id="rendered">
    <h3>2. Render the chunk through aLatteX explicitly</h3>
    <p>
        aLatteXDemoLatte takes a chunk name, pulls the chunk, and runs it
        through this plugin's engine - a second, deliberate Latte pass. Its
        output is spliced into the page by Evolution CMS like any other
        snippet output, so EVO tags the chunk produced still resolve.
    </p>

    [[aLatteXDemoLatte?
        &chunk=`aLatteXDemoCard`
        &vars=`{"title":"Rendered from a chunk","body":"The chunk was compiled by a second Latte pass, started from PHP inside a snippet.","tags":["latte","chunk","snippet"]}`
    ]]

    <h4>The same snippet with inline Latte instead of a chunk</h4>
    [[aLatteXDemoLatte?
        &code=`<p>Inline Latte, document {$id}: {$pagetitle|upper}</p>`
        &vars=`{}`
    ]]
</section>

<section id="inverted">
    <h3>3. Keep the loop in the template and the markup in the chunk</h3>
    <p>
        Usually the better answer. Latte does the logic where it is cheap, and
        the chunk stays what a chunk is good at - a lump of markup Evolution
        CMS can fill in.
    </p>

    {var $rows = [
        ['title' => 'First', 'body' => 'Built by the template.'],
        ['title' => 'Second', 'body' => 'No second pass needed.'],
        ['title' => 'Third', 'body' => 'Chunk stays plain markup.'],
    ]}

    <div class="alx-grid">
        {foreach $rows as $row}
            <article class="alx-card">
                <h4>{$row['title']}</h4>
                <p>{$row['body']}</p>
                {* A snippet call assembled per iteration. *}
                {evoSnippet('aLatteXDemoList', [
                    'items' => $row['title'] . '||' . $iterator->counter,
                    'class' => 'alx-row-' . $iterator->counter,
                ])}
            </article>
        {/foreach}
    </div>
</section>

<section id="snippet-output">
    <h3>Snippet output is not Latte either</h3>
    <p>
        The rule is symmetrical. A snippet that returns
        <code>&#123;$pagetitle&#125;</code> produces those characters, because
        no Latte pass remains to interpret them. If a snippet needs to build
        markup from a template, it must render that template itself, the way
        aLatteXDemoLatte does.
    </p>
    <p>What a snippet returns is still parsed for EVO tags, though:</p>
    [[aLatteXDemoNested? &depth=`1`]]
</section>

<main class="alx-content">[*content*]</main>

{{aLatteXDemoFooter}}

</body>
</html>
